<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dugdamme</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 640 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dugdamme was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a> in the 7th century BCE, known from Assyrian texts and called Lygdamis by Greek writers. He led raids across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> and, according to Greek tradition, burning the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, and threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>. Assyrian texts record that he fell ill and died during a campaign against Assyria around 640 BCE; his son Sandakshatru succeeded him.</p></div>
